.\" =============================================================================
.TH TSFS-LS 1 2026-10-13 "tsfs" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
tsfs\-ls \- list directory contents, tagging the retention of each entry inside TSFS mounts
.SH SYNOPSIS
.SY ls
.RI [ option ...]
.RI [ file ...]
.YS
.SY tsfs\-ls
.RI [ option ...]
.RI [ file ...]
.YS
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B tsfs\-ls
is a wrapper around the system
.BR ls (1)
for systems that use
.BR tsfs (1).
It is installed as
.I /usr/bin/ls
by default, the system
.B ls
being renamed
.IR /usr/bin/ls.orig .
It never lists anything itself: every listing is produced by the original
.BR ls ,
so it accepts all the options of the system
.B ls
with their usual meaning.
.PP
The original
.B ls
is executed with the unchanged command line
.RB ( execv (3))
when:
.IP \(bu 2
no
.I file
operand is inside a TSFS mount (filesystem type
.B fuse.tsfs
in
.IR /proc/self/mountinfo );
without operand, the current directory is the target;
.IP \(bu 2
standard output is not a terminal (pipes, redirections, scripts get exactly the
output of the system
.BR ls );
.IP \(bu 2
the environment variable
.B TSFS_LS
is set to
.BR off ;
.IP \(bu 2
the options include
.BR \-\-help ", " \-\-version ", " \-\-zero ", " \-\-dired " or " \-D .
.PP
Otherwise (at least one operand inside a TSFS mount, and a terminal or
.BR TSFS_LS=always ),
the original
.B ls
runs with the user's options followed by
.B "\-l \-\-dired"
(inserted before a
.BR \-\- ,
so that a later
.BR \-C ", " \-x " or " \-m
cannot cancel the long format), then the operands, its standard output captured.
The dired trailer
.RB ( //DIRED//
gives the byte offsets of every listed name,
.B //SUBDIRED//
those of the directory headers; GNU coreutils and uutils coreutils produce it)
tells exactly which entry is on which line, so sorting,
.BR \-a ", " \-A ", " \-R ", " \-I ", " \-\-hide ", " \-\-ignore ,
.BR \-\-time\-style ", " \-i ", " \-s ", " \-n ", " \-h ,
several operands and glob patterns keep their meaning.
Each entry then receives a tag:
.IP \(bu 2
with a long format
.RB ( \-l ", " \-n ", " \-g ", " \-o ", " \-\-format=long " or " verbose ),
the lines of the original
.B ls
are kept (without the two-space dired indentation) and the tag is appended to each
entry line;
.IP \(bu 2
with any other format, each entry is printed on its own line followed by its tag
(a column layout cannot carry it); the
.B total
lines are dropped, the directory headers and blank lines are kept.
.PP
Options that take their value as the next argument
.RB ( \-I ", " \-T ", " \-w ", " \-\-block\-size ", " \-\-format ", " \-\-hide ,
.BR \-\-ignore ", " \-\-indicator\-style ", " \-\-quoting\-style ", " \-\-sort ,
.BR \-\-tabsize ", " \-\-time ", " \-\-time\-style ", " \-\-width )
are recognized, so their value is not taken for an operand.
If the output of the original
.B ls
has no dired trailer (an
.B ls
without
.B \-\-dired
support), it is printed unchanged, without tags.
With
.BR filesystem.retention_xattrs: " false"
in the daemon configuration, every entry is tagged
.BR "RETENTION UNKNOWN (filesystem.retention_xattrs is false)" .
.SS Tags
The tag is computed from the read-only extended attributes served by the TSFS
daemon
.RB ( user.tsfs.state ", " user.tsfs.rule ", " user.tsfs.expires ", " user.tsfs.created );
dates are in local time
.RI ( YYYY\-MM\-DD " " HH:MM ).
.TP
.RI "created " date ", filter " rule ", deleted " date " (in " remaining )
TSFS will delete the file at that date; the remaining time is shown in days and
hours, hours and minutes, minutes, or seconds.
.TP
.RI "created " date ", filter " rule ", deletion pending (expired " date )
The file is already expired; the garbage collector will delete it soon.
.TP
.B UNFILTERED
TSFS will never delete the entry: no expiring rule matches (retention
.B never
or default
.BR never ),
an exclude rule matches, or it is a directory, a symbolic link or a special file.
.PP
.B created
is the birth time of the backing file
.RB ( statx (2)
.BR btime )
when the backing filesystem reports it, else its modification time.
The deletion date is computed from the timestamp of the rule (the modification
time by default, the birth time with
.BR "timestamp: birth" ),
so
.B created
can differ from the date the expiry is based on: a file whose modification time
was set in the past shows
.B created
today but can already be expired.
.PP
Output example on a terminal (uutils coreutils 0.8.0 as the original
.BR ls ):
.PP
.in +4n
.EX
$ ls \-l rec
total 20
\-rw\-r\-\-r\-\- 1 dev dev    6 Oct 13 12:30 clip.mp4  created 2026\-10\-13 12:30, filter rec, deleted 2026\-10\-14 12:30 (in 1d 0h)
\-rw\-r\-\-r\-\- 1 dev dev    2 Oct 13 12:30 hold.keep  UNFILTERED
\-rw\-r\-\-r\-\- 1 dev dev    2 Oct 11 12:30 old.mp4  created 2026\-10\-13 12:30, filter rec, deletion pending (expired 2026\-10\-12 12:30)
\-rw\-r\-\-r\-\- 1 dev dev    2 Oct 13 12:30 sp ace.mp4  created 2026\-10\-13 12:30, filter rec, deleted 2026\-10\-14 12:30 (in 1d 0h)
drwxr\-xr\-x 2 dev dev 4096 Oct 13 12:30 sub  UNFILTERED
$ ls \-R rec
rec:
clip.mp4  created 2026\-10\-13 12:30, filter rec, deleted 2026\-10\-14 12:30 (in 1d 0h)
hold.keep  UNFILTERED
old.mp4  created 2026\-10\-13 12:30, filter rec, deletion pending (expired 2026\-10\-12 12:30)
sp ace.mp4  created 2026\-10\-13 12:30, filter rec, deleted 2026\-10\-14 12:30 (in 1d 0h)
sub  UNFILTERED

rec/sub:
deep.mp4  created 2026\-10\-13 12:30, filter rec, deleted 2026\-10\-14 12:30 (in 1d 0h)
.EE
.in
.PP
Colors are not shown in TSFS listings (the original
.B ls
writes to a pipe), and control characters in names are printed as
.BR ? .
.SS The original ls
The original
.B ls
is, in this order:
.BR $TSFS_REAL_LS ,
.I /usr/bin/ls.orig
(the build option
.B TSFS_SYSTEM_LS
followed by
.BR .orig ),
then the first
.B ls
of the
.B PATH
that is not the wrapper itself (recognized by its marker string
.BR TSFS\-LS\-WRAPPER\-v2 ).
It always runs with
.I argv[0]
set to
.BR ls :
multi-call binaries such as uutils coreutils select the utility from it.
.SH OPTIONS
All the options of the system
.BR ls (1).
.B tsfs\-ls
has no option of its own.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B TSFS_LS
.B off
makes every call run the original
.B ls
with the unchanged command line;
.B always
tags TSFS listings even when standard output is not a terminal.
.TP
.B TSFS_REAL_LS
Path of the original
.B ls
to run, tried first (it must be executable).
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /usr/bin/ls
The wrapper, at a default root installation (the path follows the build option
.BR TSFS_SYSTEM_LS ).
.TP
.I /usr/bin/ls.orig
The system
.BR ls ,
renamed when the wrapper was installed: a regular file (GNU coreutils) or a
symbolic link (for example to the uutils coreutils multi-call binary), kept as is.
It can be run directly.
.TP
.I /usr/local/bin/tsfs\-ls
The wrapper under its own name (always installed).
.TP
.I /proc/self/mountinfo
Read to find the TSFS mounts.
.SH EXIT STATUS
The exit status of the original
.BR ls .
2 when the original
.B ls
cannot be found or run
.RB ( "ls: the original ls cannot be found (/usr/bin/ls.orig missing; set TSFS_REAL_LS)" ).
.SH NOTES
By default,
.B "cmake \-\-install"
run as root, without
.BR DESTDIR ,
renames the system
.I /usr/bin/ls
to
.I /usr/bin/ls.orig
.RB ( rename (2),
no copy; a symbolic link is renamed as is), then installs the wrapper as
.I /usr/bin/ls
(copied to a temporary name, then renamed: the replacement is atomic).
A staged or package installation
.RB ( DESTDIR )
or an installation by another user never touches it.
Installing again only refreshes the wrapper; if a coreutils reinstallation or
upgrade put the system
.B ls
back over the wrapper, it is renamed over the stale
.IR ls.orig .
If
.I /usr/bin/ls
is the wrapper but
.I /usr/bin/ls.orig
is missing, the installation fails and asks for
.BR "apt install \-\-reinstall coreutils" .
Build with
.B \-DTSFS_REPLACE_SYSTEM_LS=OFF
to keep the system
.B ls
.RB ( tsfs\-ls
stays installed under its own name).
.PP
The
.B uninstall
target of the build tree renames
.I /usr/bin/ls.orig
back to
.I /usr/bin/ls
(identical original; a symbolic link keeps its target).
If
.I ls.orig
is missing, it prints a warning with the
.B coreutils
reinstall command; if
.I /usr/bin/ls
is already the system one, it removes the stale
.IR ls.orig .
.PP
.I /usr/bin/ls
belongs to the
.B coreutils
package:
.B "dpkg \-\-verify coreutils"
reports it while the wrapper is in place, and a coreutils upgrade puts the system
.B ls
back over the wrapper; install TSFS again to re-apply it.
.PP
The retention attributes exist only when the daemon runs with
.B filesystem.retention_xattrs
true (the default); without them every entry is tagged
.BR UNFILTERED .
They are not returned by
.BR listxattr (2);
read them by name with
.BR "getfattr \-n user.tsfs.state" " \fIfile\fR."
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ls (1),
.BR tsfs (1),
.BR getfattr (1),
.BR getxattr (2).
.PP
The user guide
.RI ( user\-guide.md ,
"Retention of a file" and "The TSFS ls replacing the system ls"), installed in the
TSFS documentation directory (by default
.IR /usr/local/share/doc/tsfs/user/ ).
